**How it works:**

- `[a-zA-Z0-9]+` matches maximal runs of letters and digits; every other character (spaces, punctuation, apostrophes) acts as a separator. So `"don't"` splits into `"don"` and `"t"`.
- `.lower()` makes the counting case-insensitive.
- `Counter` tallies occurrences; wrapping in `dict()` gives a plain dict.
- An empty or punctuation-only string produces no matches, so `findall` returns `[]` and the result is `{}`.

**Quick checks:**

```python
word_frequencies("The cat, the CAT!")   # {'the': 2, 'cat': 2}
word_frequencies("don't")                # {'don': 1, 't': 1}
word_frequencies("")                     # {}
word_frequencies("...!!! ---")           # {}
word_frequencies("a1 A1 b2")             # {'a1': 2, 'b2': 1}
```

A note on Unicode: `[a-zA-Z0-9]` treats only ASCII letters/digits as word characters, so accented letters (é, ñ) count as separators. If you need Unicode-aware "letters and digits," swap the pattern to `r'[^\W_]+'` with `re.findall(pattern, text.lower(), re.UNICODE)` — but that also joins on the underscore differently, so the ASCII version above matches your stated spec most literally.

